13:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section battle-flow-section","id":"battle-flow","children":["$","$L1d",null,{"guide":{"date":"September 17, 1862","place":"The northern Antietam battlefield near Sharpsburg, Maryland, from the Joseph Poffenberger farm south through the East Woods and Miller’s Cornfield to the Mumma farm and Dunker Church plateau","result":"Between about 7:30 and 9 a.m., the United States XII Corps relieved Hooker’s exhausted I Corps and drove D. H. Hill’s reinforcements from the East Woods, Cornfield, Mumma farm, and ground east of the Hagerstown Turnpike. Maj. Gen. Joseph K. F. Mansfield was mortally wounded as his inexperienced corps deployed. Brig. Gen. Alpheus S. Williams then directed the corps while Brig. Gen. George S. Greene exploited the collapse of Garland’s brigade, turned Colquitt’s flank, and reached the Dunker Church plateau. The advance put the Confederate left in grave danger, but the wider breakthrough continues in the next episode","facts":[{"label":"Episode scope","value":"The XII Corps attack, about 7:30–9 a.m.","note":"The episode begins as Hooker’s and Jackson’s formations are spent, follows Mansfield’s corps through the East Woods and Cornfield, and ends with Greene near the Dunker Church."},{"label":"Opening balance","value":"Hooker about 2,600 casualties; Jackson about 3,000","note":"These are the episode’s estimates for the first ninety minutes of fighting. Both commands were badly damaged, with neither side holding a decisive advantage at about 7:30 a.m."},{"label":"McClellan’s next reinforcement","value":"Two divisions of Sumner’s II Corps","note":"McClellan ordered Sumner forward at about 7:20 a.m. but kept one division east of Antietam Creek until Morell’s V Corps division could replace it in reserve."},{"label":"Uncommitted Federal wing","value":"Burnside’s IX Corps still had no order to attack","note":"The quiet on the Confederate right allowed Lee to send troops north toward the crisis on his left."},{"label":"XII Corps strength","value":"About 7,200 men in the episode’s estimate","note":"Roughly half were entering battle for the first time, including five recently mustered regiments."},{"label":"Mansfield’s tenure","value":"Two days in command of XII Corps","note":"The 58-year-old career engineer had taken command on September 15 and was leading his first important Civil War field command."},{"label":"Approach to battle","value":"Columns of companies with repeated halts","note":"The corps marched from its bivouac near the Poffenberger farms. Officers struggled to move and deploy the many new regiments while the men repeatedly tried to make breakfast."},{"label":"Mansfield’s mortal wound","value":"Shot in the chest in the East Woods","note":"Mansfield initially believed the 10th Maine was firing into retreating Federal troops. After recognizing the Confederates in front, he was hit and carried to the rear; he died on September 18."},{"label":"128th Pennsylvania","value":"118 casualties in the episode’s account","note":"The new regiment lost Col. Samuel L. Croasdale and had Lt. Col. William W. Hammersly wounded while attempting to deploy. It then advanced into the Cornfield before breaking under Confederate fire."},{"label":"Change of command","value":"Alpheus S. Williams took over XII Corps","note":"Williams received Hooker’s instructions under fire and placed Crawford on the right, Gordon in the center, and Greene on the left."},{"label":"D. H. Hill’s reinforcement","value":"About 3,400 men in three brigades","note":"Ripley was already south of the Cornfield while Colquitt and McRae advanced north from the Confederate center."},{"label":"Gordon’s stand","value":"2nd Massachusetts, 3rd Wisconsin, and 27th Indiana","note":"These regiments held the higher Miller pasture north of the Cornfield and fought Colquitt’s brigade advancing through the corn."},{"label":"Lost Order casualties","value":"Mitchell and Bloss wounded; Capt. Peter Kop mortally wounded","note":"The three men of the 27th Indiana had handled the lost copy of Lee’s Special Orders No. 191 after its discovery near Frederick."},{"label":"Replacement systems","value":"Confederate replacements joined veteran regiments","note":"The episode contrasts this practice with the Federal tendency to organize new regiments. Experienced comrades could train Confederate conscripts, but McRae’s brigade showed that the system did not eliminate battlefield panic."},{"label":"Garland’s brigade breaks","value":"A perceived flank threat triggered a stampede","note":"Still shaken by its rout and the death of Brig. Gen. Samuel Garland at South Mountain, McRae’s brigade broke in the East Woods when the large 28th Pennsylvania approached its flank."},{"label":"Greene’s attacking force","value":"About 1,700 men in two brigades","note":"Greene’s official report gives 1,727 men remaining under his immediate command after his third brigade was detached to the right."},{"label":"Tyndale’s pivot","value":"East Woods to a west-facing line","note":"Lt. Col. Hector Tyndale wheeled the 28th Pennsylvania and three Ohio regiments against Colquitt’s exposed flank at the north edge of the Cornfield."},{"label":"Close-range volley","value":"About 30 yards in the episode’s account","note":"Tyndale’s line struck Colquitt while the Confederates were focused on Gordon’s troops in the Miller pasture."},{"label":"Colquitt’s loss","value":"About half the brigade","note":"Colquitt’s official report states that he lost one-half of the men in the ranks during the unequal fight."},{"label":"Stainrook’s turning movement","value":"A wider sweep to Smoketown Road and the Mumma farm","note":"Greene sent Col. Henry J. Stainrook’s brigade south and west, forcing the remaining Confederates from their foothold in the East Woods."},{"label":"Captured Confederate guns","value":"A two-gun section sent forward by Stephen D. Lee","note":"The episode says men of the 28th Pennsylvania captured the section after the Confederate withdrawal began."},{"label":"Dunker Church plateau","value":"Stephen D. Lee abandoned the morning artillery position","note":"Greene’s advance through the East Woods and Mumma farm threatened the plateau, forcing Lee’s batteries to pull back beyond the West Woods."},{"label":"Federal high-water mark in scope","value":"125th Pennsylvania entered the West Woods near the church","note":"By close to 9 a.m., XII Corps held the Cornfield, East Woods, Mumma farm, and the ground east of the turnpike."},{"label":"Northern-field toll","value":"About 8,000 casualties by 9 a.m. in the episode’s estimate","note":"The episode treats the losses as roughly evenly divided between United States and Confederate forces after three hours of combat."},{"label":"Immediate opportunity","value":"The Confederate left appeared close to collapse","note":"Hooker believed he might capture Lee’s army or drive it into the Potomac, but the XII Corps advance was unsupported and Sumner’s attack is reserved for the next episode."},{"label":"Recommended book","value":"“Landscape Turned Red” by Stephen W. Sears","note":"The hosts recommend Sears’s narrative history of the Maryland Campaign and Battle of Antietam."},{"label":"Transcript normalizations","value":"Poffenberger, XII Corps, Morell, Mumma, Dunker Church, McRae, de Rosset, Greene, Tyndale, Stainrook, Croasdale, Hammersly, and Peter Kop","note":"The automated transcript produces forms including “Poffenberg,” “Morrill,” “Dunkirk Church,” “McCrae,” “Green,” “Tyndall,” “Steynrick,” “Crossdale,” “Hammersley,” and “Peter Copp.” This guide uses the standard historical spellings."}],"maps":[{"title":"XII Corps enters the northern battlefield","caption":"Mansfield’s corps moved south from the Poffenberger farms along and west of Smoketown Road. Crawford deployed toward the Hagerstown Turnpike, Gordon reinforced the Miller pasture and Cornfield, and Greene moved through the East Woods. D. H. Hill sent Ripley, Colquitt, and McRae north to rebuild the Confederate line. Positions and movements are generalized. This map is schematic and not to scale.","source":"Antietam National Battlefield, Alpheus S. Williams’s and D. H. Hill’s Official Reports, Antietam Battlefield Board atlas, and episode transcript; see sources below.","image":"/images/ep193-xii-corps-arrives.svg","alt":"Schematic map of the northern Antietam battlefield showing the Twelfth Corps moving south through the East Woods and toward Miller’s Cornfield while three brigades of D. H. Hill’s division move north."},{"title":"Greene turns the Confederate line, about 8–9 a.m.","caption":"After McRae’s brigade broke in the East Woods, Tyndale pivoted west and struck Colquitt’s flank at the Cornfield while Gordon engaged from the north. Stainrook swept toward the Mumma farm. The Confederate brigades withdrew, Stephen D. Lee abandoned the Dunker Church plateau, and the 125th Pennsylvania crossed the turnpike near the church. Movements are generalized and compressed. This map is schematic and not to scale.","source":"Antietam National Battlefield, George S. Greene’s, Alpheus S. Williams’s, Alfred Colquitt’s, Duncan McRae’s, and D. H. Hill’s Official Reports, Antietam Battlefield Board atlas, and episode transcript; see sources below.","image":"/images/ep193-greene-turning-movement.svg","alt":"Schematic tactical map showing Tyndale pivoting from the East Woods into Colquitt’s flank, Stainrook advancing toward the Mumma farm, and Confederate troops retreating toward the West Woods and Dunker Church."},{"title":"Maj. Gen. Joseph K. F. Mansfield","caption":"A Brady studio portrait of the XII Corps commander, made between 1861 and 1862. Mansfield was mortally wounded while directing new regiments in the East Woods and died the next day.","source":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division, Liljenquist Family Collection; see sources below.","image":"/images/ep193-joseph-mansfield.jpg","alt":"Full-length studio portrait of Major General Joseph K. F. Mansfield in United States Army uniform with a sword."}],"moments":[{"time":"5:07","audioTime":307,"title":"The shattered northern battlefield is recapped","detail":"At about 7:30 a.m., Hooker and Jackson have lost roughly 5,600 men between them, yet the lines remain close to their dawn positions."},{"time":"6:27","audioTime":387,"title":"McClellan finally orders Sumner forward","detail":"Hood’s counterattack prompts an order for II Corps to reinforce the northern battle, though one division must remain behind until relieved."},{"time":"7:51","audioTime":471,"title":"Burnside remains idle","detail":"No order has yet sent IX Corps against the Confederate right, allowing Lee to shift troops toward his endangered left."},{"time":"8:39","audioTime":519,"title":"D. H. Hill feeds in Ripley’s brigade","detail":"Ripley reaches the pasture south of the Cornfield too late to join Hood’s charge but in time to meet the Federal pursuit."},{"time":"9:35","audioTime":575,"title":"XII Corps approaches from the north","detail":"Mansfield’s mission is now to support Hooker’s wrecked formations and prevent a Confederate breakthrough."},{"time":"9:49","audioTime":589,"title":"The march repeatedly stalls","detail":"Stephen Sears’s account describes columns halting, men lighting breakfast fires, and orders arriving before the coffee can boil."},{"time":"11:15","audioTime":675,"title":"Wilder Dwight writes from the saddle","detail":"The lieutenant colonel of the 2nd Massachusetts tells his mother that Hooker is heavily engaged and that he is well so far."},{"time":"11:31","audioTime":691,"title":"Hooker asks for help everywhere","detail":"Mansfield learns that I Corps needs support in the West Woods, East Woods, and Cornfield, then rides back to hurry his corps."},{"time":"11:56","audioTime":716,"title":"The corps’ inexperience is measured","detail":"About half of XII Corps’ 7,200 men have never fought, and five new regiments have been in service for less than a month."},{"time":"12:30","audioTime":750,"title":"Mansfield’s first field command is introduced","detail":"After forty years in the Regular Army, the 58-year-old engineer has led XII Corps for only two days."},{"time":"13:08","audioTime":788,"title":"Green regiments need personal guidance","detail":"Williams uses a fence to align one regiment and explains how simple maneuvers could throw raw officers and men into confusion."},{"time":"14:06","audioTime":846,"title":"Mansfield challenges the 10th Maine’s fire","detail":"Believing friendly troops remain in the East Woods, Mansfield orders the veterans to stop until they point out the Confederates."},{"time":"14:38","audioTime":878,"title":"Mansfield is mortally wounded","detail":"A bullet strikes him in the chest; he is carried through the fire to an ambulance and dies the following day."},{"time":"14:56","audioTime":896,"title":"The 128th Pennsylvania tries to deploy","detail":"The new regiment loses its colonel and second-in-command during an unfamiliar movement from column into line."},{"time":"15:57","audioTime":957,"title":"The 128th attacks through the corn","detail":"Its remaining commander chooses movement over standing under fire, but the regiment breaks after emerging from the Cornfield with 118 casualties."},{"time":"16:27","audioTime":987,"title":"Williams takes command","detail":"After Mansfield falls, Williams confers with Hooker, Gibbon, and Meade under fire and assumes direction of XII Corps."},{"time":"18:23","audioTime":1103,"title":"Hill rebuilds the Confederate line","detail":"About 3,400 men in the brigades of Ripley, Colquitt, and McRae form from the turnpike toward the East Woods."},{"time":"19:23","audioTime":1163,"title":"Colquitt meets Gordon in the Cornfield","detail":"Three Federal regiments stand on the elevated Miller pasture while Colquitt’s Georgians and Alabamians advance through the corn."},{"time":"20:18","audioTime":1218,"title":"The Lost Order trio’s Antietam fates are recalled","detail":"The episode notes that Barton Mitchell and John Bloss were wounded and that their company commander Peter Kop was also a battle casualty; later records identify Kop’s wound as mortal."},{"time":"21:18","audioTime":1278,"title":"The replacement systems are compared","detail":"The episode explains how Confederate conscripts joined veteran regiments while Federal states more often raised entirely new units."},{"time":"22:56","audioTime":1376,"title":"McRae’s brigade panics","detail":"Men fearing another flank attack break in the East Woods and carry most of Garland’s brigade toward Sharpsburg."},{"time":"24:10","audioTime":1450,"title":"Robert Gould Shaw crosses the wrecked field","detail":"Shaw describes Gordon’s brigade moving among Confederate dead and wounded and Federal soldiers offering water and aid."},{"time":"27:36","audioTime":1656,"title":"Greene seizes the opening","detail":"The veteran division commander sends Tyndale through the East Woods and pivots the brigade west toward Colquitt’s flank."},{"time":"28:56","audioTime":1736,"title":"Tyndale delivers a close volley","detail":"The 28th Pennsylvania and three Ohio regiments fire into Colquitt’s line at roughly 30 yards, then charge into the Cornfield."},{"time":"29:57","audioTime":1797,"title":"Stainrook makes the wider turn","detail":"Greene’s second brigade reaches Smoketown Road and the Mumma farm, forcing Hood’s remnants from the East Woods."},{"time":"30:22","audioTime":1822,"title":"The 28th Pennsylvania captures guns","detail":"A two-gun Confederate section sent forward by Stephen D. Lee cannot withdraw before Federal soldiers reach it."},{"time":"30:57","audioTime":1857,"title":"Stephen D. Lee abandons the plateau","detail":"Greene’s advance forces the Confederate artillery battalion away from its commanding position east of the Dunker Church."},{"time":"31:12","audioTime":1872,"title":"The 125th Pennsylvania reaches the church","detail":"The new regiment crosses the Hagerstown Turnpike and enters the West Woods near and north of the Dunker Church."},{"time":"31:23","audioTime":1883,"title":"XII Corps turns the battle","detail":"By close to 9 a.m., Mansfield’s corps has rescued I Corps and set the conditions for a Federal breakthrough on the northern field."},{"time":"32:21","audioTime":1941,"title":"The three-hour cost reaches 8,000","detail":"The episode estimates that casualties are about equally divided across the devastated Cornfield, woods, pasture, and turnpike."},{"time":"33:54","audioTime":2034,"title":"Hooker believes victory is within reach","detail":"With Greene near the church and the 125th Pennsylvania in the West Woods, Hooker thinks the Confederate army might be captured or driven into the Potomac."},{"time":"34:40","audioTime":2080,"title":"The book recommendation closes the episode","detail":"The hosts recommend Stephen W. Sears’s “Landscape Turned Red: The Battle of Antietam.”"}],"command":[{"side":"United States","color":"#315f8c","branches":[{"commander":"Maj. Gen. George B. McClellan","command":"Army of the Potomac","subordinates":"Ordered two divisions of Sumner’s II Corps toward the northern fight while retaining one division east of the creek; had not yet ordered Burnside’s IX Corps to attack"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Joseph Hooker","command":"I Corps and northern wing","subordinates":"Held the battered northern line and directed Mansfield and then Williams to reinforce the West Woods, Cornfield, and East Woods"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Joseph K. F. Mansfield","command":"XII Corps","subordinates":"Led the corps south from the Poffenberger farms and was mortally wounded while posting troops in the East Woods"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. Alpheus S. Williams","command":"1st Division, then XII Corps","subordinates":"Succeeded Mansfield and directed Crawford on the right, Gordon in the center, and Greene on the left"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. Samuel W. Crawford / Col. Joseph F. Knipe","command":"1st Brigade, Williams’s division","subordinates":"Deployed new and veteran regiments toward the East Woods, Miller pasture, and Hagerstown Turnpike"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. George H. Gordon","command":"3rd Brigade, Williams’s division","subordinates":"Held the corps center; three regiments engaged Colquitt from the Miller pasture north of the Cornfield"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. George S. Greene","command":"2nd Division, XII Corps","subordinates":"Exploited the Confederate collapse, turned Colquitt’s flank, cleared the East Woods, and advanced to the Dunker Church plateau"},{"commander":"Lt. Col. Hector Tyndale","command":"1st Brigade, Greene’s division","subordinates":"Pivoted west from the East Woods with the 28th Pennsylvania and three Ohio regiments and struck Colquitt at close range"},{"commander":"Col. Henry J. Stainrook","command":"2nd Brigade, Greene’s division","subordinates":"Made the wider turn through the East Woods toward Smoketown Road and the Mumma farm"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Edwin V. Sumner","command":"II Corps","subordinates":"Marched from east of Antietam Creek with two divisions; his arrival and attack continue in the next episode"}]},{"side":"Confederacy","color":"#963b31","branches":[{"commander":"Gen. Robert E. Lee","command":"Army of Northern Virginia","subordinates":"Shifted forces from the quiet Confederate right toward the crisis on the left"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Thomas J. “Stonewall” Jackson","command":"Confederate left wing","subordinates":"Held the northern battlefield with badly damaged divisions after the attacks and counterattacks of I Corps"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. D. H. Hill","command":"Hill’s division, Confederate center","subordinates":"Sent roughly 3,400 men in Ripley’s, Colquitt’s, and Garland’s brigades north to reconstruct the line"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. Roswell S. Ripley","command":"Ripley’s brigade","subordinates":"Occupied the pasture south of the Cornfield and met Federal troops following Hood’s retreat"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. Alfred H. Colquitt","command":"Colquitt’s brigade","subordinates":"Attacked through the Cornfield against Gordon before Tyndale struck the brigade’s flank"},{"commander":"Col. Duncan K. McRae","command":"Garland’s brigade","subordinates":"Guarded the East Woods flank, but the brigade panicked and retreated when it perceived Greene’s approach"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. John Bell Hood","command":"Hood’s division","subordinates":"Rallied the remnants of his exhausted division behind the Dunker Church as XII Corps advanced"},{"commander":"Col. Stephen D. Lee","command":"Artillery battalion","subordinates":"Sent a two-gun section forward and then withdrew his batteries from the Dunker Church plateau before Greene’s advance"}]}],"people":[{"name":"Joseph K. F. Mansfield","role":"XII Corps commander mortally wounded while personally directing troops in the East Woods","side":"United States","image":"/images/ep193-joseph-mansfield.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2021636784/"},{"name":"Alpheus S. Williams","role":"Division commander who succeeded Mansfield and directed XII Corps through the rest of the attack","side":"United States","source":"https://antietam.aotw.org/exhibit.php?exhibit_id=335"},{"name":"George S. Greene","role":"Led the XII Corps division that cleared the East Woods and reached the Dunker Church plateau","side":"United States","source":"https://antietam.aotw.org/exhibit.php?exhibit_id=134"},{"name":"George H. Gordon","role":"Held the Miller pasture with veteran regiments while Colquitt advanced through the Cornfield","side":"United States","source":"https://antietam.aotw.org/exhibit.php?exhibit_id=335"},{"name":"Hector Tyndale","role":"Led Greene’s first brigade in the pivot and close-range attack on Colquitt’s flank","side":"United States","source":"https://antietam.aotw.org/exhibit.php?exhibit_id=134"},{"name":"Henry J. Stainrook","role":"Led Greene’s second brigade on the wider turn toward the Mumma farm","side":"United States","source":"https://antietam.aotw.org/exhibit.php?exhibit_id=134"},{"name":"Samuel L. Croasdale","role":"Colonel of the new 128th Pennsylvania, killed as the regiment attempted to deploy","side":"United States","source":"https://antietam.aotw.org/officers.php?officer_id=914"},{"name":"Barton W. Mitchell","role":"27th Indiana corporal who had found Lee’s lost order and was wounded in the Cornfield fighting","side":"United States","source":"https://www.in.gov/history/state-historical-markers/find-a-marker/find-historical-markers-by-county/indiana-historical-markers-by-county/pvt.-mitchell/"},{"name":"Peter Kop","role":"27th Indiana company commander who received the lost order from Mitchell and was mortally wounded at Antietam","side":"United States","source":"https://antietam.aotw.org/officers.php?officer_id=3671"},{"name":"Robert Gould Shaw","role":"Captain in the 2nd Massachusetts whose letter describes the dead, wounded, and prisoners around the Cornfield","side":"United States"},{"name":"D. H. Hill","role":"Confederate division commander who sent three brigades north from the center to support Jackson","side":"Confederacy","source":"https://antietam.aotw.org/exhibit.php?exhibit_id=32"},{"name":"Alfred H. Colquitt","role":"Brigade commander who engaged Gordon in the Cornfield before Greene’s troops struck his flank","side":"Confederacy","source":"https://antietam.aotw.org/exhibit.php?exhibit_id=368"},{"name":"Duncan K. McRae","role":"Colonel commanding Garland’s brigade when it broke in the East Woods","side":"Confederacy","source":"https://antietam.aotw.org/exhibit.php?exhibit_id=239"},{"name":"William L. de Rosset","role":"Colonel of the 3rd North Carolina whose account describes teaching a new soldier to aim beneath the smoke","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"John Bell Hood","role":"Division commander who rallied his scattered survivors behind the Dunker Church","side":"Confederacy","source":"https://antietam.aotw.org/exhibit.php?exhibit_id=31"}],"losses":[{"name":"Maj. Gen. Joseph K. F. Mansfield","side":"United States","role":"Commander of XII Corps","outcome":"Mortally wounded by a rifle ball in the chest while directing troops in the East Woods; carried to a field hospital and died on September 18 after only two days in corps command."},{"name":"Col. Samuel L. Croasdale","side":"United States","role":"Commander of the 128th Pennsylvania, Crawford’s brigade","outcome":"Killed as his inexperienced regiment attempted to deploy from column into line near the East Woods and Miller pasture."},{"name":"Lt. Col. William W. Hammersly","side":"United States","role":"Second-in-command of the 128th Pennsylvania","outcome":"Wounded moments after Croasdale was killed, deepening the command confusion before the regiment entered the Cornfield."},{"name":"Capt. Peter Kop","side":"United States","role":"Company F, 27th Indiana, Gordon’s brigade","outcome":"Mortally wounded by a stray bullet later on September 17 after surviving the morning’s close-range fighting; he died at a field hospital on September 24. Earlier in the campaign, Barton Mitchell and John Bloss had brought Lee’s lost order to him."}],"gallery":[{"image":"/images/ep193-xii-corps-arrives.svg","alt":"Schematic map of XII Corps approaching the Cornfield and East Woods.","caption":"Mansfield’s inexperienced corps entered a battlefield already crowded with broken I Corps formations while D. H. Hill sent three Confederate brigades north.","source":"Antietam National Battlefield, Official Reports, Antietam Battlefield Board atlas, and episode transcript."},{"image":"/images/ep193-greene-turning-movement.svg","alt":"Schematic map of Greene’s turning movement from the East Woods to the Dunker Church plateau.","caption":"Tyndale and Stainrook exploited the collapse in the East Woods, struck Colquitt’s flank, and forced the Confederate line back beyond the church plateau.","source":"Antietam National Battlefield, Official Reports, Antietam Battlefield Board atlas, and episode transcript."},{"image":"/images/ep193-joseph-mansfield.jpg","alt":"Brady studio portrait of Maj. Gen. Joseph K. F. Mansfield.","caption":"Mansfield took command of XII Corps two days before Antietam and was mortally wounded while leading its first advance.","source":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division, Liljenquist Family Collection."}],"sources":[{"label":"Official Civil War Podcast episode 193 page","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.org/2017/04/30/civil-war-podcast-episode-193/"},{"label":"Antietam National Battlefield: The East Woods","url":"https://www.nps.gov/places/the-east-woods.htm"},{"label":"Antietam National Battlefield: Joseph K. F. Mansfield","url":"https://www.nps.gov/people/joseph-k-mansfield.htm"},{"label":"Antietam National Battlefield: XII Corps order of battle","url":"https://home.nps.gov/anti/learn/historyculture/org-cht-12-corps.htm"},{"label":"Antietam National Battlefield: Cornfield Trail introduction and map","url":"https://home.nps.gov/anti/planyourvisit/cornfield-trail-guide.htm"},{"label":"Antietam National Battlefield: Cornfield Corner of Death","url":"https://www.nps.gov/anti/planyourvisit/cornfield-trail-guide-stop-6.htm"},{"label":"Antietam National Battlefield: generals killed and wounded","url":"https://home.nps.gov/anti/learn/historyculture/6generals.htm"},{"label":"U.S. Army Center of Military History: Antietam staff ride guide","url":"https://history.army.mil/portals/143/Images/Publications/catalog/35-3-1.pdf"},{"label":"Official Records, Series I, Volume XIX, Part I","url":"https://ehistory.osu.edu/books/official-records/027"},{"label":"Primary source: Alpheus S. Williams’s official report","url":"https://antietam.aotw.org/exhibit.php?exhibit_id=335"},{"label":"Primary source: George S. Greene’s official report","url":"https://antietam.aotw.org/exhibit.php?exhibit_id=134"},{"label":"Primary source: D. H. Hill’s official report","url":"https://antietam.aotw.org/exhibit.php?exhibit_id=32"},{"label":"Primary source: Alfred H. Colquitt’s official report","url":"https://antietam.aotw.org/exhibit.php?exhibit_id=368"},{"label":"Primary source: Duncan K. McRae’s official report","url":"https://antietam.aotw.org/exhibit.php?exhibit_id=239"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Atlas of the battlefield of Antietam","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/map05000006/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Maj. Gen. Joseph K. F. Mansfield portrait","url":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2021636784/"},{"label":"Official podcast book-recommendations list","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.org/book-recommendations/"}]}}]}]
14:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section maps-section","id":"maps","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"Maps"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"maps-grid","children":[["$","figure","Maj. Gen. Joseph K. F. Mansfield",{"className":"reference-map","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"map-frame static-map-frame","children":["$","a",null,{"className":"map-image-link","href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/images/ep193-joseph-mansfield.jpg","target":"_blank","rel":"noreferrer","aria-label":"Open Maj. Gen. Joseph K. F. Mansfield at full size","children":["$","img",null,{"src":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/images/ep193-joseph-mansfield.jpg","alt":"Full-length studio portrait of Major General Joseph K. F. Mansfield in United States Army uniform with a sword.","width":1600,"height":1200,"loading":"lazy"}]}]}],["$","figcaption",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Maj. Gen. Joseph K. F. Mansfield"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"A Brady studio portrait of the XII Corps commander, made between 1861 and 1862. Mansfield was mortally wounded while directing new regiments in the East Woods and died the next day."}],["$","span",null,{"className":"map-actions","children":[["$","a",null,{"href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/images/ep193-joseph-mansfield.jpg","target":"_blank","rel":"noreferrer","children":["Open full-size map ",["$","$Le",null,{"ref":"$undefined","iconNode":"$d:props:children:1:props:children:0:props:children:1:props:children:2:props:iconNode","className":"lucide-arrow-up-right","size":13}]]}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division, Liljenquist Family Collection; see sources below."}]]}],"$undefined"]}]]}]]}]]}]
15:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section","id":"people","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"People and command"}],[[["$","link","css:/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/page.m4AAmS8w.css",{"rel":"stylesheet","precedence":"vite-rsc/importer-resources","href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/page.m4AAmS8w.css","data-rsc-css-href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/page.m4AAmS8w.css"}],["$","link","css:/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/index.B0pjVDS7.css",{"rel":"stylesheet","precedence":"vite-rsc/importer-resources","href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/index.B0pjVDS7.css","data-rsc-css-href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/index.B0pjVDS7.css"}],["$","link","css:/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/command-trees.KCNQiAeH.css",{"rel":"stylesheet","precedence":"vite-rsc/importer-resources","href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/command-trees.KCNQiAeH.css","data-rsc-css-href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/command-trees.KCNQiAeH.css"}],"$undefined"],[["$","div",null,{"className":"command-grid reference-command","children":[["$","section","United States",{"className":"command-column #315f8c","children":[["$","h3",null,{"children":"United States"}],[["$","div","Army of the Potomac-Maj. Gen. George B. McClellan",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Army of the Potomac"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Maj. Gen. George B. McClellan"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Ordered two divisions of Sumner’s II Corps toward the northern fight while retaining one division east of the creek; had not yet ordered Burnside’s IX Corps to attack"}]]}],["$","div","I Corps and northern wing-Maj. Gen. Joseph Hooker",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"I Corps and northern wing"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Maj. Gen. Joseph Hooker"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Held the battered northern line and directed Mansfield and then Williams to reinforce the West Woods, Cornfield, and East Woods"}]]}],["$","div","XII Corps-Maj. Gen. Joseph K. F. Mansfield",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"XII Corps"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Maj. Gen. Joseph K. F. Mansfield"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Led the corps south from the Poffenberger farms and was mortally wounded while posting troops in the East Woods"}]]}],["$","div","1st Division, then XII Corps-Brig. Gen. Alpheus S. Williams",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"1st Division, then XII Corps"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Brig. Gen. Alpheus S. Williams"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Succeeded Mansfield and directed Crawford on the right, Gordon in the center, and Greene on the left"}]]}],["$","div","1st Brigade, Williams’s division-Brig. Gen. Samuel W. Crawford / Col. Joseph F. Knipe",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"1st Brigade, Williams’s division"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Brig. Gen. Samuel W. Crawford / Col. Joseph F. Knipe"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Deployed new and veteran regiments toward the East Woods, Miller pasture, and Hagerstown Turnpike"}]]}],["$","div","3rd Brigade, Williams’s division-Brig. Gen. George H. Gordon",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"3rd Brigade, Williams’s division"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Brig. Gen. George H. Gordon"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Held the corps center; three regiments engaged Colquitt from the Miller pasture north of the Cornfield"}]]}],["$","div","2nd Division, XII Corps-Brig. Gen. George S. Greene",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"2nd Division, XII Corps"}],"$L1e","$L1f"]}],"$L20","$L21","$L22"]]}],"$L23"]}],"$L24"]]]}]
16:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section","id":"losses","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"Important leaders killed or wounded"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"loss-list","children":[["$","article","Maj. Gen. Joseph K. F. Mansfield",{"children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"side union","children":"United States"}],["$","h3",null,{"children":"Maj. Gen. Joseph K. F. Mansfield"}],["$","p",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":["Commander of XII Corps","."]}]," ","Mortally wounded by a rifle ball in the chest while directing troops in the East Woods; carried to a field hospital and died on September 18 after only two days in corps command."]}]]}],["$","article","Col. Samuel L. Croasdale",{"children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"side union","children":"United States"}],["$","h3",null,{"children":"Col. Samuel L. Croasdale"}],["$","p",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":["Commander of the 128th Pennsylvania, Crawford’s brigade","."]}]," ","Killed as his inexperienced regiment attempted to deploy from column into line near the East Woods and Miller pasture."]}]]}],["$","article","Lt. Col. William W. Hammersly",{"children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"side union","children":"United States"}],["$","h3",null,{"children":"Lt. Col. William W. Hammersly"}],["$","p",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":["Second-in-command of the 128th Pennsylvania","."]}]," ","Wounded moments after Croasdale was killed, deepening the command confusion before the regiment entered the Cornfield."]}]]}],["$","article","Capt. Peter Kop",{"children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"side union","children":"United States"}],["$","h3",null,{"children":"Capt. Peter Kop"}],["$","p",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":["Company F, 27th Indiana, Gordon’s brigade","."]}]," ","Mortally wounded by a stray bullet later on September 17 after surviving the morning’s close-range fighting; he died at a field hospital on September 24. Earlier in the campaign, Barton Mitchell and John Bloss had brought Lee’s lost order to him."]}]]}]]}]]}]
